      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "The bite out of a circle"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "A chord cuts a circle into two parts called segments. Finding their area needs a sector and a triangle."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "60 degrees: equilateral",
          "annulus sector",
          "combined figures",
          "major segment",
          "quadrant case",
          "segment",
          "segment = sector - triangle",
          "split into parts"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "A segment is the region between a chord and the arc it cuts off.\nArea of a minor segment = area of the sector minus area of the triangle formed by the two radii and the chord.\nFor a right angle at the centre with radius r, the sector area is pi r^2/4 and the triangle area is r^2/2.\nFor a 60 degree angle, the triangle is equilateral and its area is root 3/4 times r squared.\nFor a major segment, find the area of the circle minus the minor segment.\nMany problems combine sectors, squares and triangles: flower beds, logos, clock hands, windscreen wipers.\nFor a wiper sweep, area swept = sector of the larger radius minus sector of the smaller radius.\nDraw and label the figure first, and break it into pieces whose areas you know."
        }
      },
